84.4,"_gate_insider_own":0.56256,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"T-Mobile US, Inc., together with its subsidiaries, provides wireless communications services in the United States, Puerto Rico, and the United States Virgin Islands. The company offers voice, messaging, and data services to postpaid, prepaid, and wholesale and other services customers. It also provides wireless devices, including smartphones, wearables, tablets, home broadband gateways, headsets, and other mobile communication devices, as well as accessories; financing through equipment installment plans; reinsurance for device insurance policies and extended warranty contracts. The company offers services under the T-Mobile, Metro by T-Mobile, and Mint Mobile brands through its owned and operated retail stores, customer care channels, national retailers, and its websites, as well as through T-Mobile, Metro by T-Mobile, and Mint Mobile apps. It also sells devices to dealers and other third-party distributors for resale through independent third-party retail outlets and various third-party websites. It has 700 MHz wireless spectrum licenses. The company was founded in 1994 and is headquartered in Bellevue, Washington. T-Mobile US, Inc. operates as a subsidiary of Deutsche Telekom AG.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Srinivasan  Gopalan | CEO, President & Director | Age 55 | Compensation: $8.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 75K people. Revenue per employee of $1.2M ranks in the top quartile among Communication Services peers, with revenue growing 23%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $240K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $11K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 7\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["T-Mobile US, Inc. is a large-cap communication services business with a $180.0B market cap on $88.3B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +4% over the past three years and +11% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 8% against a 7% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The business throws off $18.0B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 20 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.54\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 2.5\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 4.7\u00d7.","Management returns a meaningful 7.8% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 56% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.5) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Insiders are overwhelmingly selling (100% sell ratio) \u2014 the people who know the business want less of it","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","ratio":0.0},{"text":"Advertising-cycle exposure and content/regulatory risk \u2014 a single platform policy shift or ad-spend contraction cascades through the P&L quickly.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Cord-cutting acceleration and audience fragmentation \u2014 legacy distribution bundles unravel faster than subscription revenue can replace them.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"AI-generated content commoditisation \u2014 as creation costs collapse toward zero, content libraries lose pricing power and differentiation narrows.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 12% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading at a 56% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"How Are Premium Plans and Account Growth Lifting Cash Flow Targets at TMUS (NASDAQ:TMUS)?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/communication/how-are-premium-plans-and-account-growth-lifting-cash-flow-targets-at-tmus-nasdaqtmus","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794575280.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.71,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"TMUS vs. NOK: Which Telecom Stock is the Better Buy Now?","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3029282/tmus-vs-nok-which-telecom-stock-is-the-better-buy-now?cid=CS-ZC-FT-analyst_blog%7Cmost_popular_stocks-3029282","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794572280.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.44,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"3,466 T-Mobile US, Inc. $TMUS Shares Bought by Retirement Systems of Alabama","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/13/3466-t-mobile-us-inc-tmus-shares-bought-by-retirement-systems-of-alabama.html","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794566725.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.912,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"T-Mobile US, Inc. $TMUS Stock: Maryland State Retirement & Pension System Raises Position","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/13/t-mobile-us-inc-tmus-stock-maryland-state-retirement-pension-system-raises-position.html","date":"2026-11-13","tim